From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 13:07:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 88/88] fix(witness): make the worktree reap survive its own
 SIGKILL (gcp-mves)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The reaper runs as the witness pre_start, which gascity SIGKILLs at [session]
setup_timeout. `git worktree remove` drops the worktree's .git file and git's
admin entry BEFORE unlinking the tree, so a kill in that window left a
directory that was present on disk, absent from `git worktree list`, and
absent from the log — the failure report sits downstream of the kill. Gate 1
enumerates from `git worktree list`, so the reaper could never look at such a
directory again. winnow's first armed run stripped 40 worktrees that way and
reported none of them (winnow-h0n2o), and the residue is still on this box.

Removal now RENAMES FIRST: `mv <wt> <wt>.reaping` (a sibling rename, so
rename(2), so atomic), then `git worktree prune` to drop the admin entry that
now points nowhere, then `rm -rf`. A kill before the rename leaves the
worktree intact and still enumerable; a kill at or after it leaves a directory
whose suffix says what it is. The marker is the evidence — residue is never
inferred from an mtime, which cannot tell a stripped tree from a slow one.
`git worktree remove` is not called at all any more; it cannot be made safe
here, because what has to be survived is the kill, not the command.

Candidates are now the union of the admin list and a walk of the rig's own
worktree tree, because a directory with no admin entry is exactly what gate 1
structurally cannot see. Scan roots come from the admin list itself — an agent
home stays registered even when every one of its per-bead children has been
stripped — so the walk is a glob over known directories, never a `find`. The
test is ABSENCE FROM THE ADMIN LIST rather than a shape inside the directory:
keying on `.reaping` plus "no .git" would leave a third shape (a worktree git
can still administer but has forgotten) unowned for the same reason the first
two were. Widening what is looked at does not widen what is removed — residue
takes the same gates.

Gate 3 is the one that cannot apply unchanged, and the trap is not obvious:
`git -C <stripped-dir> status` does NOT fail. With no .git of its own git
walks UP and answers about the AGENT HOME (measured: `?? worktrees/`), so a
naive residue gate would read a verdict about a directory it never inspected.
The ceiling is pinned at the candidate's own parent and the reported toplevel
and common-dir must be the candidate itself and this rig. Residue git can
still administer takes the ordinary clean-tree gate; residue it cannot is
recorded as UNEVALUABLE, naming why, and stands on gates 2 and 4. Residue with
no bead is kept outright: gate 5 carries that path and needs a `git rev-parse
HEAD` the directory cannot answer either, so no evidence would exist at all.

The destructive call also had no re-validation at the point of use: the bead
status came from one bulk read at the top of the run, the roster from a read
after it, and the clean-tree check from before gate 5, all while the rig is
live. The three gates that can change underneath a removal are re-checked
immediately before the rename, and anything that cannot be RE-CONFIRMED is a
refusal rather than a removal. This does not bring back the N+1 that cost
winnow its witness for 26h (gcp-ntbf): `git status` is per-worktree and local
as it already was, while the bead and roster renewals are one bulk pair for
the whole cycle, taken lazily at the first removal.

Finally the promotion criterion, in agents/witness/agent.toml and in
mol-witness-patrol's copy of it. Both stated the staged-rollout soak as
sufficient. It is not, and this is the part worth generalising: a dry run
never calls the destructive path at all, so no soak — however complete its
coverage — is evidence about whether a removal COMPLETES. winnow's witness
verified the criterion honestly and still lost the rig. Both now say the soak
evidences SELECTION only, and name the two remaining preconditions (this bead
landed AND gascity-3z7d resolved, not either). The stale gc-zxxy rationale is
replaced rather than deleted: that reason expired, the hold did not.

Validation. RED first, recorded verbatim on the bead: seven new cases failed
against the unchanged script for the stated reasons, including a fixture that
reproduces winnow's artifact end to end by stalling `git worktree remove` half
done and SIGKILLing the reaper inside that window. Green after: 24 cases in
test_polecat_worktree_reap.sh, test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh, the whole
gastown/tests/test_*.sh sweep, and shellcheck -S warning on both shell files.

Checked read-only against the real residue on this box, which is the evidence
that matters most: 40 stripped directories across two lane trees, of which 17
are under `views/` — so a walk keyed on the `polecats` tree name would have
recovered 23 and orphaned the rest, and gcp-elv3's shape-not-name rule is
reused rather than re-derived. From winnow's real rig root the admin list
holds 22 worktrees and ZERO per-bead ones, so today's reaper enumerates
nothing there. The two shape predicates, extracted verbatim from the patched
script, match all 40 with no rejections, and all 40 are reachable from the 18
scan roots the admin list supplies. The dotted sub-bead ids survive the leaf
whitelist, `winnow-grif7.17.2` included.

Two guards outside the bead's four asks had to move with it.
test_gastown_pack_assets.sh requires every `record worktree_*` event to have a
row in mol-witness-patrol's log-review table, so the four new events are
documented there. And test_bead_status_is_read_in_one_bulk_query pinned the
bead read at exactly ONE call; a live cycle now takes two, both bulk and both
once per cycle. Pinning the literal 1 forbids the second read without
expressing the invariant it stands for, so the case now runs its fixture at
two sizes and asserts the counts MATCH — flat in the number of worktrees,
which is what gcp-ntbf actually bought — and caps them at 2, so the N+1 cannot
return disguised as a re-check.

Two budget cases were given headroom (2s/3s to 6s) after one of them failed
under load in a full-suite run: a cycle spends ~1s reaching the bead read, so
a 2s budget could land on `skipped`, which is a different and also correct
verdict, not the one under test. Any budget below the 20s stall still produces
the timeout being asserted.

Follow-up, deliberately not done here: the 40 directories on winnow are not
cleaned up by this commit. The reaper is disarmed on every rig under the
mayor's standing ruling, so they are removed only once an operator arms it,
which now requires gascity-3z7d as well. This makes them enumerable, gated and
logged; it does not arm anything.

